    "manufacturer": "London Transport",
    "collection": "London Transport Museum",
    "movement": "Information Design",
    "wikiTitle": "Tube_map",
    "description": "Beck, an engineering draughtsman — not a graphic designer — proposed replacing the geographically accurate Underground map with a schematic using only horizontal, vertical and 45-degree lines. London Transport rejected it. When printed as a trial, public demand made it permanent. Beck was paid five guineas.",
    "significance": "Beck understood that underground passengers don't need geography — they need topology. The map sacrifices literal truth for functional truth. Every metro system in the world now follows his principle. The most influential information design ever produced, made by a man the organisation barely acknowledged.",
    "connections": [
      {
        "id": 14,
        "type": "method",
        "reason": "Beck drew a diagram so clear that anyone could navigate London. Tschichold wrote rules so clear that anyone could typeset Penguin. Neither trusted talent — both trusted systems. The diagram and the style guide: design that works without a designer in the room."
      },
      {
        "id": 55,
        "type": "lineage",
        "reason": "Beck's 1933 map stripped London's geography to a diagram — Johnston's sans-serif lettering was essential to that abstraction. Thirty-seven years later, Vignelli applied the same logic to New York: abstract the geography, systematise the typography, trust the diagram. Two cities, two centuries of transit, one conviction that the map IS the system."
      },
      {
        "id": 829,
        "type": "method",
        "reason": "Beck, an engineering draughtsman, replaced geographic accuracy with schematic clarity — horizontal, vertical, and 45-degree lines making the Underground navigable at the cost of spatial truth. Du Bois, a sociologist, replaced the conventional bar chart with a spiral diagram — radial proportion making the growth of Black land ownership in Georgia legible in a way no standard statistical format could. Both invented new visual grammars because existing conventions couldn’t carry their argument."
      }
    ],
