    "wikiTitle": null,
    "description": "Cast glass sculpture exploiting the material's optical properties through precise control of thickness and curvature. The form channels light through its translucent mass, creating internal reflections that shift with viewing angle. Libensky's mastery of glass chemistry allows for gradual opacity transitions that sculpt light itself.",
    "significance": "Libensky does not shape glass — he shapes light, using glass as the medium through which light is sculpted. Thickness controls opacity, curvature controls refraction, and the form exists primarily as an optical instrument. The boundary between sculpture and lens dissolves because Libensky's material knowledge makes the distinction irrelevant.",
    "connections": [
      {
        "id": 3,
        "type": "material",
        "reason": "Libenský casts glass so that light transforms as it passes through varying thicknesses — the sculpture IS the light, channelled by mass. The Parentesi positions a spotlight on a cable — the lamp delivers the light, guided by gravity. Both make light the design material, but Libenský sculpts it through solid glass and Castiglioni aims it through empty space."
      },
      {
        "id": 15,
        "type": "argument",
        "reason": "Piano and Rogers bolt every pipe, truss, and escalator to the Pompidou's exterior (1977) — the building as diagram of its own making. Libenský casts glass into monolithic geometric forms that reveal nothing of the kiln process — the sculpture as mystery, technique concealed within frozen light. Architecture that shows you everything and glass that tells you nothing."
      },
      {
        "id": 370,
        "type": "zeitgeist",
        "reason": "Libensky's Arcus 1 (1991): cast glass as architectural sculpture — the material's optical depth as the content. Clarke's Gone Fishing (2000): ceramic as narrative object — the vessel's cultural reference as the content. Two makers in the 1990s asserting that craft knowledge produces design intelligence that factories can't replicate."
      }
    ],
