    "wikiTitle": null,
    "description": "A dining chair that references the geometric vocabulary of Dutch architect H.P. Berlage through simplified rectangular forms and right-angled construction. The chair reduces seating to essential geometric elements—seat, back, and legs—arranged in orthogonal relationships that echo Berlage's architectural rationalism. Despite its minimal appearance, the proportions and angles are carefully calibrated for ergonomic comfort within the geometric constraints.",
    "significance": "Hutten's chair is a Berlage building at furniture scale — orthogonal, rational, geometrically disciplined. The historical reference is not decorative quotation but structural inheritance: the same right-angled logic that organised Berlage's architecture organises the seat, back, and legs. Heritage as method, not as ornament.",
    "connections": [
      {
        "id": 410,
        "type": "zeitgeist",
        "reason": "Sexy Relaxy exaggerates — it swallows you whole. The Berlage Chair subtracts — it gives you a geometric frame and nothing more. Hutten in 2002, arguing with himself across two chairs about whether comfort is indulgence or discipline."
      },
      {
        "id": 24,
        "type": "method",
        "reason": "Berlage's chair applies architectural proportional systems to furniture — the whole form governed by geometric ratios derived from building design. Jacobsen's Egg applies sculptural intuition to furniture — the whole form governed by how foam curves around a sitting body. Both bridge architecture and furniture, but Berlage through proportion and Jacobsen through ergonomics."
      },
      {
        "id": 343,
        "type": "sameProblem",
        "reason": "Morrison's Thinking Man's Chair (1986) reduces a garden chair to welded steel rod — geometric minimalism as quiet intelligence. Hutten's Berlage Chair (2004) reduces a dining chair to right-angled planes — geometric minimalism as architectural homage. Both arrive at rectilinear austerity, but Morrison subtracts until nothing is left while Hutten builds from Berlage's orthogonal vocabulary."
      }
    ],
