    "movement": "Biomorphism",
    "wikiTitle": "Orgone_(lounge)",
    "description": "A horizontal chaise longue in moulded lacquered fibreglass, its hourglass-shaped shell resting on three cone-shaped legs. The form was inspired by surfboard construction techniques and Wilhelm Reich's orgone energy theories. Unlike the hand-beaten aluminium of the Lockheed Lounge, the Orgone was designed for series production by Cappellini, making Newson's biomorphic language accessible at industrial scale. Available in a range of high-gloss lacquer colours.",
    "significance": "Newson translating his gallery language into industrial production without losing formal ambition. The Cappellini fibreglass version made the Orgone available at production scale — the same biomorphic form that existed as a limited aluminium edition for collectors could now be lacquered in candy colours and sold through furniture shops. It proved that Newson's sculptural instinct survived the transition from hand-beaten metal to moulded composite.",
    "connections": [
      {
        "id": 88,
        "type": "material",
        "reason": "Newson's Orgone uses moulded fibreglass in high-gloss lacquer — industrial boat-building material recast as luxury furniture. Rams's RT 20 uses perforated metal as a functional speaker grille; Newson uses fibreglass as a sculptural surface."
      },
      {
        "id": 595,
        "type": "lineage",
        "reason": "The Embryo (1988): neoprene skin, tubular steel legs, biomorphic curves executed in wetsuit material. The Orgone (1993): moulded fibreglass chaise on three cone legs, surfboard construction scaled to furniture. Both pursue organic form through industrial materials."
      },
      {
        "id": 613,
        "type": "method",
        "reason": "Newson’s Orgone Lounge (1993): a moulded fibreglass chaise on three cone legs, surfboard construction scaled to furniture, available in high-gloss lacquer colours. His Felt Chair (1993): a moulded fibreglass shell on a single splayed aluminium leg, named for how it looks rather than what it’s made of. Same designer, same year, same manufacturer (Cappellini), same material — the Orgone reclines the body, the Felt Chair cradles it. Two arguments for biomorphic fibreglass, delivered simultaneously."
      }
    ],
