    "collection": "In production",
    "movement": "Democratic Scandinavian-French Design",
    "wikiTitle": null,
    "description": "A comprehensive outdoor furniture system — chairs, benches, tables, loungers — made from hot-dip galvanised steel tube and flat bar. The diagonal cross-bracing pattern that gives the collection its name provides structural rigidity without added weight. Every piece stacks. The steel construction allows year-round outdoor use without degradation. Designed for HAY's accessible price point.",
    "significance": "The Bouroullecs proving they can design for democratic production as effectively as for Vitra's premium market. It demonstrates that designing affordable outdoor furniture for a mass market is as legitimate an ambition as designing gallery editions — and arguably harder, because every gram of material and every weld has a cost consequence.",
    "connections": [
      {
        "id": 6,
        "type": "method",
        "reason": "The Bouroullecs' Palissade (2015): powder-coated steel tubes forming outdoor furniture through geometric repetition — the section is the system. Ponti's Superleggera (1957): triangular ash legs forming a dining chair through structural reduction — the joint is the achievement. Efficiency through geometry, in steel and in wood."
      },
      {
        "id": 14,
        "type": "method",
        "reason": "The Bouroullecs' Palissade uses one steel section profile across an entire outdoor furniture collection — bench, chair, table, all from the same extrusion. Tschichold uses one typographic system across Penguin's entire publishing output — novel, guide, classic, all from the same grid. Single-system design generating entire families."
      },
      {
        "id": 819,
        "type": "argument",
        "reason": "The Bouroullecs’ Palissade collection uses hot-dip galvanised steel tube to make outdoor furniture that is entirely rigid — the diagonal cross-bracing resists flex, and the material weathers rather than wears. Their Slow Chair does the opposite: a knitted textile stretched over tubular aluminium that flexes and gives under the sitter’s weight, becoming more comfortable over time. Two furniture systems from the same designers — one resists the body, the other negotiates with it."
      }
    ],
