    "description": "A woven wall hanging in cotton and silk using a complex colour-and-weave structure where the pattern emerges from the interaction of thread colour and weave direction rather than from any applied image. The textile is its own content — no picture, no motif, no representation. Albers treated the loom as a technology for generating visual complexity from simple binary decisions: over or under, warp or weft.",
    "significance": "Albers elevated weaving from 'women's work' at the Bauhaus to a rigorous formal discipline equal to painting or architecture. Her wall hangings prove that textiles can be abstract, systematic, and intellectually demanding without borrowing from any other art form. The loom is not a craft tool — it's a binary computer that happens to produce cloth.",
