    "description": "A dome-shaped pavilion whose primary structure is a CNC-wound silk thread scaffold over which 6,500 live silkworms were released. The worms filled gaps in the scaffold with their own silk, responding to light gradients: denser silk in darker areas, sparser silk where light was brighter. The pavilion is co-designed by algorithm, robot, and living organism. The silkworms die naturally after completing their work.",
    "significance": "The Silk Pavilion dissolves the boundary between design, fabrication, and biology. The designer sets initial conditions — the scaffold geometry, the light gradients — and then surrenders control to organisms whose behaviour is the manufacturing process. It's the most radical proposition in contemporary design: that the maker might not be human, and that design might be a negotiation between species.",
