    "description": "A music stand carved from a single stack-laminated block of walnut. The flat reading surface grows organically from a sculptural base — there is no joint between stand and support, no visible construction logic. The grain flows continuously from floor to music rest. Castle's technique: glue up a block of wood large enough to contain the form, then carve away everything that isn't the music stand.",
    "significance": "Castle collapses the distinction between furniture and sculpture by refusing the joint. Where conventional furniture is assembled — legs attached to seats, tops fastened to bases — Castle's music stand is subtracted from a solid block. It proves that furniture can be carved rather than constructed, and that wood can behave like stone. The American craft tradition's answer to European industrial design.",
