    "wikiTitle": "Paris_M%C3%A9tro_entrances",
    "description": "Cast-iron entrance canopies and railings for the Paris Métro designed as organic structures — stems, tendrils, and seed-pod lamp standards that grow from the pavement like metallic plants. The ironwork was cast from standardised modular components that could be assembled in different configurations for different station sizes. Guimard designed 141 entrances; 86 survive. The Abbesses station retains a complete glass-and-iron canopy.",
    "significance": "Guimard solved the problem of giving a brand-new transit system an instant identity by making the entrances unmistakable — you don't read the sign, you recognise the organism. The iron plants mark their stations the way a church spire marks a parish. That the organic forms were achieved through modular industrial casting, not handwork, is the hidden radicalism: Art Nouveau meeting mass production.",
