    "description": "A Y-shaped vegetable peeler with a fat, oval, black rubber handle featuring flexible fins that grip the palm without requiring tight grasp. Sam Farber watched his wife Betsey, who had arthritis, struggle with a conventional metal peeler and commissioned Smart Design to create a kitchen tool that anyone could use. The Santoprene rubber handle moulds to varying hand sizes and grip strengths. The blade is sharp stainless steel with a built-in potato eye remover.",
    "significance": "The Good Grips peeler proved that designing for disability produces better products for everyone. Farber didn't market it as an assistive device — he sold it as a premium kitchen tool. The line launched with fifteen products and built OXO into a $300 million brand. It's the founding case study of inclusive design: the moment the profession understood that constraints are generative, not limiting.",
