    "collection": "MoMA, New York",
    "movement": "Poetic Design",
    "wikiTitle": null,
    "description": "A single low-voltage halogen bulb fitted with two handmade goose-feather wings, attached by a simple wire clip. The wings are real feathers — not moulded, not printed, not simulated. The bulb perches on the end of a red cable like a bird on a wire. Maurer hand-selected feathers for each production unit. The technology is industrial; the material is biological; the combination is impossible to categorise.",
    "significance": "Lucellino proved that poetry and engineering are not opposed — the most technically precise lamp Maurer had made was also the most tender. A lightbulb with wings shouldn't work as a design proposition, yet it does because Maurer treats absurdity with absolute seriousness. Every feather is real, every bulb is calibrated, every wire is measured. The joke is also the product.",
    "connections": [
      {
        "id": 645,
        "type": "lineage",
        "reason": "Maurer's Bulb Lamp (1966) puts a lightbulb inside a lightbulb — the source as Pop subject. Lucellino (1992) puts wings on a lightbulb — the source as animate creature. Twenty-six years between two treatments of the naked bulb: one frames it, the other releases it."
      },
      {
        "id": 798,
        "type": "method",
        "reason": "Maurer's Lucellino (1992) grafts goose feathers onto a bare bulb — adding organic material to industrial light. Arai's Melted-Off textile (1987) dissolves metallic yarn from fabric — removing industrial material from organic cloth. Both transform their medium by crossing the boundary between natural and industrial, but Maurer adds while Arai subtracts."
      },
      {
        "id": 324,
        "type": "method",
        "reason": "Studio Drift's Fragile Future (2007) embeds real dandelion seeds in an LED matrix — biological material as lighting component. Maurer's Lucellino (1992) clips real goose feathers to a halogen bulb — biological material as lighting component. Both refuse synthetic imitation, insisting on the real organism."
      }
    ],
