    "origin": "Germany",
    "manufacturer": "Ingo Maurer GmbH",
    "collection": "Die Neue Sammlung, Munich",
    "movement": "Poetic Design",
    "wikiTitle": null,
    "description": "A chandelier consisting of a stainless-steel frame from which hang 80 sheets of Japanese paper and tracing paper on thin stainless-steel wires, each sheet clipped at its corner. Forty sheets carry Maurer's own handwriting, drawings, or printed imagery; forty are left blank for the owner to write on, draw on, or replace. Five halogen bulbs illuminate the paper cloud from within. The chandelier is always unfinished — it is completed by whoever lives with it.",
    "significance": "Zettel'z dissolves authorship. Maurer designs half the lamp and gives the other half away — the owner's handwriting, children's drawings, grocery lists, love letters become part of the light fixture. It's the most radical proposition in production lighting: a manufactured object that deliberately invites its own modification. The lamp gets more personal over time rather than less.",
    "connections": [
      {
        "id": 681,
        "type": "lineage",
        "reason": "Lucellino (1992) grafts biological material onto a bulb — feathers as poetic addition. Zettel'z (1997) grafts biographical material onto a frame — handwriting as poetic addition. Maurer's progression from nature to culture, both methods of making an industrial light source intimate."
      },
      {
        "id": 604,
        "type": "method",
        "reason": "Enzo Mari's Autoprogettazione (1974) gives away furniture plans for anyone to build — the designer surrenders control of making. Maurer's Zettel'z (1997) gives away blank paper for anyone to inscribe — the designer surrenders control of meaning. Both are produced industrially but completed democratically."
      },
      {
        "id": 409,
        "type": "argument",
        "reason": "Wanders's Set Up Shades (2000) treats the lampshade as a decorative surface designed by the professional — pattern as authored statement. Maurer's Zettel'z (1997) treats the lampshade as a blank surface designed by the owner — paper as unauthored invitation. Two Dutch and German approaches to who finishes a light."
      }
    ],
