    "movement": "Deconstructivism",
    "wikiTitle": "Vitra_fire_station",
    "description": "A fire station for the Vitra furniture campus in Weil am Rhein, built from raw reinforced concrete with walls that lean, tilt, and intersect at acute angles. The building looks like it is about to move — walls becoming ramps, roof becoming wall, ground plane tilting upward. It was Hadid's first completed building, after fifteen years of unbuilt projects and competition wins. The fire service used it for three years before moving to a larger facility; it now serves as an exhibition space.",
    "significance": "The Vitra Fire Station proved that Hadid's paintings were buildable. For fifteen years, critics had dismissed her angular, gravity-defying compositions as art rather than architecture. This small building on a furniture campus — raw concrete, no cladding, no comfort — silenced them. That it stopped functioning as a fire station almost immediately is irrelevant; it functions permanently as proof that space can be destabilised without collapsing.",
    "connections": [
      {
        "id": 657,
        "type": "argument",
        "reason": "Zumthor's Therme Vals (1996) builds architecture from the weight of stone — the building settles into its mountain. Hadid's Vitra Fire Station (1993) builds architecture from the tension of concrete — the building launches from its ground. Two 1990s buildings with opposite relationships to gravity: one sinks, the other lifts."
      },
      {
        "id": 15,
        "type": "lineage",
        "reason": "Piano and Rogers' Pompidou (1977) externalises structure to liberate interior space — technology on display. Hadid's Vitra Fire Station (1993) internalises tension to destabilise interior space — geometry on attack. Both reject the neutral container, but the Pompidou is generous where the Fire Station is aggressive."
      },
      {
        "id": 520,
        "type": "method",
        "reason": "Hadid's Nova Shoes apply her architectural angles to the body — wearable deconstructivism. The Vitra Fire Station applies her architectural angles to a building — inhabitable deconstructivism. Same formal language at two scales: the shoe and the structure, both making solid ground feel unstable."
      }
    ],
