    "movement": "New Japanese Architecture",
    "wikiTitle": null,
    "description": "A museum and research centre whose entire structure is built from a three-dimensional lattice of cypress wood sticks joined without nails or screws — using only the cidori interlocking toy joint, a traditional Japanese wooden puzzle. The 6,000 sticks create a porous screen that filters light, blurs the boundary between interior and exterior, and can be disassembled and reassembled. The building is a toy scaled up to architecture.",
    "significance": "Kuma demonstrated that the most advanced structural thinking can come from the simplest material intelligence. The cidori joint — a children's puzzle — becomes a building system. No metal fasteners, no adhesive, no permanent connections. The building is technically temporary: it could be taken apart stick by stick. It argues that Japanese wood joinery, far from being nostalgic craft, is the most progressive building technology available.",
    "connections": [
      {
        "id": 637,
        "type": "method",
        "reason": "Rietveld's Red Blue Chair (1917) assembles battens so that no piece touches another at its end — every joint is a crossing. Kuma's GC Prostho Museum (2010) assembles cypress sticks so that every joint is an interlocking puzzle — every crossing is a lock. Both treat joinery as the design's entire content: the joint IS the architecture."
      },
      {
        "id": 658,
        "type": "zeitgeist",
        "reason": "Ando's Church of the Light (1989) reduces Japanese architecture to concrete and subtracted light — Western material, Eastern spatial thought. Kuma's GC Prostho Museum (2010) reduces Japanese architecture to wooden sticks and filtered light — traditional material, contemporary spatial thought. Two Japanese architects with opposite material strategies, both achieving transcendence through reduction."
      },
      {
        "id": 659,
        "type": "method",
        "reason": "Chareau's Maison de Verre (1932) uses glass blocks as a structural screen — light passes through but views don't. Kuma's GC Prostho Museum (2010) uses interlocking cypress sticks as a structural screen — light passes through and views dissolve. Both treat the building envelope as a filter rather than a barrier, but Chareau filters with glass while Kuma filters with wood."
      }
    ],
