    "movement": "Landscape Architecture",
    "wikiTitle": "Oslo_Opera_House",
    "description": "An opera house whose white Carrara marble and granite roof slopes directly into the Oslo fjord, creating a public landscape that anyone can walk on. The building submerges its performance spaces below ground level so that the roof becomes a civic plaza — you climb the building before you enter it. The slope is continuous: harbour water meets marble meets glass wall meets auditorium. Inside, 16,000 pieces of individually cut oak line the main hall.",
    "significance": "Snøhetta dissolved the distinction between building and ground. The Oslo Opera House is both architecture and landscape — a hill you can climb that happens to contain a 1,364-seat auditorium. It redefined what a public building owes its city: not a façade to admire from a distance but a surface to occupy. The marble roof is the most democratic gesture in recent architecture — it belongs to everyone who walks on it.",
    "connections": [
      {
        "id": 820,
        "type": "method",
        "reason": "Snøhetta's Oslo Opera House (2008) uses a sloping marble roof as a public walking surface — architecture at urban scale designed for the body's movement. Barber & Osgerby's Olympic Torch (2012) uses a perforated aluminium tube as a ceremonial carrying object — product at hand scale designed for the body's grip. Both are Norwegian/British civic designs where the surface treatment (marble slope, laser perforations) IS the design."
      },
      {
        "id": 657,
        "type": "method",
        "reason": "Zumthor's Therme Vals (1996) makes architecture you experience through temperature, touch, and sound — the body before the eye. Snøhetta's Oslo Opera House (2008) makes architecture you experience through walking, climbing, and sitting — the feet before the eye. Both prioritise the body over the photograph."
      },
      {
        "id": 561,
        "type": "material",
        "reason": "Chipperfield's Palace of Justice in Salerno (2012) and Snøhetta's Oslo Opera House (2008) both use stone as civic surface — Chipperfield wraps a court in travertine, Snøhetta slopes a roof in Carrara marble. Both argue that public buildings earn their authority through the weight and warmth of stone, not through glass and steel spectacle."
      }
    ],
