    "wikiTitle": "VIA_57_West",
    "description": "A 32-storey residential building whose form is a hybrid between a European courtyard block and a Manhattan skyscraper — a tetrahedron that rises from a full-block courtyard on the west to a sharp peak on the east. The courtyard provides light and greenery at ground level; the peak provides views of the Hudson River from the upper floors. Ingels calls it a 'courtscraper'. Every apartment gets both courtyard access and river views, which conventional towers make mutually exclusive.",
    "significance": "BIG proved that architectural typologies can be crossbred to solve problems that neither parent type addresses alone. The European courtyard gives ground-level community; the American tower gives upper-level views. VIA 57 West argues that the choice between them is a false binary — the most progressive building might be the one that refuses to choose.",
