    "wikiTitle": "Little_Sun",
    "description": "A palm-sized LED lamp powered by a solar panel on one side and shaped like a sunflower on the other. Designed for the 1.2 billion people who live without electricity. The lamp provides five hours of light from a single day's charge. It is sold at full price in wealthy markets and at subsidised cost in off-grid communities — the profit from one funds the access of the other. Eliasson designed it with engineer Frederik Ottesen.",
    "significance": "Eliasson — an artist whose installations fill Tate turbine halls — applied the same spatial intelligence to a product that fits in a hand. Little Sun proves that design for the base of the pyramid doesn't require condescension or compromise. It's as formally resolved as any MoMA acquisition but it works in a context where design magazines will never photograph it. The subsidy model is itself a design decision: the economic structure is inseparable from the object.",
