    "year": 2007,
    "discipline": "Furniture",
    "origin": "Israel / United Kingdom",
    "manufacturer": "Raw Edges Studio",
    "collection": "V&A, London",
    "movement": "Process-Led Design",
    "wikiTitle": null,
    "description": "A series of furniture — stool, bench, table — made by stacking and gluing thin plywood sheets into a block, then carving the block with a bandsaw into organic forms. The exposed layers create a topographic pattern on every surface — each cut reveals a cross-section of the laminated stack, like geological strata or the growth rings of a tree. The grain of the furniture IS its end grain: the name describes the technique.",
    "significance": "Raw Edges demonstrated that a simple workshop operation — stack, glue, cut — could generate visual complexity that no surface treatment could match. The topographic pattern isn't applied; it's revealed by the act of cutting. Every curve the bandsaw makes produces a different pattern, which means the design decisions are the cutting decisions. The process is the ornament.",
    "connections": [
      {
        "id": 663,
        "type": "method",
        "reason": "Wendell Castle's Music Stand (1964) stack-laminates a block and carves away everything that isn't furniture — subtraction from a solid mass. Raw Edges' Endgrain (2007) stack-laminates a block and carves away everything that isn't furniture — subtraction from a layered mass. Same technique, but Castle hides the laminations while Raw Edges celebrates them."
      },
      {
        "id": 670,
        "type": "zeitgeist",
        "reason": "Max Lamb's Pewter Stool (2007) and Raw Edges' Endgrain (2007) are both RCA-connected process-led designs from the same year — both make the manufacturing process the visual content of the object. Lamb's sand-cast surface records the mould; Raw Edges' topographic pattern records the cut. Both locate beauty in the trace of making."
      },
      {
        "id": 636,
        "type": "method",
        "reason": "Scholten & Baijings' Paper Porcelain (2010) preserves the paper mould's texture in fired ceramic — the process imprinted on the product. Raw Edges' Endgrain (2007) reveals the plywood stack's layers through bandsaw cutting — the process exposed in the product. Both make material process visible as surface quality."
      },
      {
        "id": 827,
        "type": "argument",
        "reason": "Raw Edges' Endgrain (2007) stacks cross-sections of wood so the end-grain pattern creates surface colour and texture — one material providing all the variation, the stacking method holding uniformity together. Jongerius's Tall Cabinet (2015) mixes wood, metal, textile, and ceramic — each material retaining its own identity, nothing unified, the proportions holding the discord together. Raw Edges achieve richness through material singularity; Jongerius achieves it through material plurality."
      }
    ],
