    "description": "A collection of furniture and accessories for IKEA using cork, stoneware, water hyacinth, and raw-edged glass — materials chosen for how they feel rather than how they photograph. The cork table is warm to touch. The stoneware mug has an unglazed rim that meets the lip differently from porcelain. The water hyacinth basket smells of the plant. Crawford's brief to IKEA was 'consider every sense, not just sight'. Every surface was tested for touch, weight, temperature, and scent.",
    "significance": "Crawford proved that IKEA — the most visually-led furniture company on earth — could produce objects designed for the body rather than the camera. Sinnerlig is the anti-Instagram collection: the cork looks boring in photographs but feels extraordinary in the hand. It argues that the most important qualities of domestic objects are the ones you can't scroll past — warmth, weight, texture, scent.",
    "connections": [
      {
        "id": 346,
        "type": "argument",
        "reason": "Morrison's Pill Stool (2006) practises Super Normal — ordinary objects so refined they disappear into daily life. Crawford's Sinnerlig (2015) practises sensory design — ordinary objects so tactile they reappear through the body. Morrison eliminates attention; Crawford redirects it. Both reject spectacle, but Morrison achieves invisibility while Crawford achieves intimacy."
      },
      {
        "id": 657,
        "type": "method",
        "reason": "Zumthor's Therme Vals (1996) designs architecture for temperature, touch, and sound — the body before the eye. Crawford's Sinnerlig (2015) designs furniture for warmth, weight, and scent — the body before the eye. Both insist that the most important design decisions are the ones that engage senses other than vision."
      },
      {
        "id": 355,
        "type": "zeitgeist",
        "reason": "Grcic's Mono Table (2002) and Crawford's Sinnerlig (2015) are both furniture designed for major manufacturers — Grcic for SCP, Crawford for IKEA. Grcic reduces to geometric precision; Crawford enriches through material warmth. Two approaches to accessible furniture: one perfects form, the other perfects feeling."
      }
    ],
