    "movement": "Post-Radical Italian Design",
    "wikiTitle": null,
    "description": "A series of furniture — shelves, tables, cabinets — constructed from rough industrial metal frames penetrated by living tree branches. The branches are real, cut, and inserted through the steel structures so that the natural and the industrial physically intersect. Some pieces include animal skins. The title translates as 'Domestic Animals' — the furniture is presented as a breed of creature that inhabits the home, neither fully natural nor fully artificial.",
    "significance": "Branzi, a founding member of Archizoom and one of Italian radical design's key theorists, used Animali Domestici to argue that post-industrial civilisation lives between nature and technology — and that furniture should embody that tension rather than resolving it. The branches will dry out, crack, and change; the metal will rust. The furniture ages. It refuses the modernist promise of timeless, unchanging form.",
    "connections": [
      {
        "id": 12,
        "type": "lineage",
        "reason": "Sottsass's Carlton (1981) attacks modernist good taste through colour and ornament — Memphis as cultural provocation. Branzi's Animali Domestici (1985) attacks modernist good taste through raw nature and rust — post-Memphis as ecological provocation. Both are Italian, both are post-modern, but Sottsass uses laminate and Branzi uses tree branches."
      },
      {
        "id": 589,
        "type": "method",
        "reason": "Bey's Tree Trunk Bench (1999) inserts a bronze backrest into a fallen tree — furniture found inside nature. Branzi's Animali Domestici (1985) inserts tree branches through steel frames — nature forced inside furniture. Both cross the boundary between organic and industrial, but Bey enters the tree while Branzi makes the tree enter the cabinet."
      },
      {
        "id": 627,
        "type": "zeitgeist",
        "reason": "Formafantasma's Cambio (2020) investigates the timber industry as a global system — wood as political material. Branzi's Animali Domestici (1985) forces raw branches through industrial steel — wood as confrontational material. Thirty-five years between two Italian practices that refuse to treat wood as neutral."
      }
    ],
