    "manufacturer": "n/a (limited edition)",
    "collection": "Cooper Hewitt, Smithsonian Design Museum",
    "movement": "Para-Conceptual Design",
    "wikiTitle": null,
    "description": "A standard IKEA lamp with a Swarovski crystal attached to the pull-chain switch. Wong bought the cheapest IKEA desk lamp available, added a single crystal, and sold it for $250. The gesture takes five seconds. The transformation is total — the crystal makes you see the IKEA lamp as if for the first time. Wong called his practice 'para-conceptual' — it exists alongside conventional design, commenting on it without abandoning the object.",
    "significance": "Wong, who died in 2010 at thirty-five, was design's closest equivalent to Marcel Duchamp. The Knock-Off Lamp proves that the smallest possible intervention — one crystal on one chain — can shift an object's entire meaning. It's a critique of luxury (a Swarovski crystal on an IKEA lamp), of authorship (Wong designed nothing, he added), and of value (the $3 lamp now sells for $250). Cooper Hewitt acquired his archive in 2025, recognising him as a defining voice of early 21st-century design.",
    "connections": [
      {
        "id": 2,
        "type": "lineage",
        "reason": "The Castiglionis' Mezzadro (1957) recontextualises a tractor seat as furniture — the readymade as design method. Wong's Knock-Off (2003) recontextualises an IKEA lamp as luxury object — the readymade as design critique. Both add almost nothing to an existing product; both change everything about how you see it."
      },
      {
        "id": 703,
        "type": "lineage",
        "reason": "Wong's Knock-Off Lamp (2003) adds a crystal to an IKEA lamp and puts quotation marks around value — para-conceptual design as critique. Abloh's Ten (2017) adds quotation marks to a Nike shoe and puts them on the runway — para-conceptual design as commerce. Wong opens the door; Abloh walks through it into a billion-dollar industry."
      },
      {
        "id": 460,
        "type": "argument",
        "reason": "Wieki Somers' High Tea Pot (2003) encases a teapot in a taxidermied animal — the domestic object made uncanny. Wong's Knock-Off Lamp (2003) encases an IKEA lamp in luxury connotation — the domestic object made aspirational. Both take familiar household products and apply a single transformation that makes them impossible to ignore."
      }
    ],
