    "wikiTitle": null,
    "description": "A poster for the Stedelijk Museum's temporary home at the former Post CS building in Amsterdam — 'Temporary Stedelijk Museum' rendered in Helvetica Bold on a white ground, each line progressively truncated so that the institution's name visually compresses as you read downward. The poster is entirely typographic: no image, no colour, no ornament. Experimental Jetset work exclusively in Helvetica, printing in one or two colours, rejecting illustration. They describe their practice as 'turning language into objects'.",
    "significance": "Experimental Jetset represent the logical endpoint of Dutch systematic graphic design — Crouwel's grid, Tschichold's discipline, and Helvetica's neutrality taken to their absolute limit. Their work proves that extreme constraint doesn't produce boring design — it produces design where every decision is visible. The MoMA acquisition of their posters and T-shirts acknowledges that a practice built on refusing to do things is itself a radical proposition.",
    "connections": [
      {
        "id": 660,
        "type": "lineage",
        "reason": "Crouwel's New Alphabet (1967) reduces type to a grid as a provocation about machine constraints. Experimental Jetset's practice (2000s) reduces type to Helvetica as a discipline about self-imposed constraints. Crouwel opens the systematic tradition; Experimental Jetset inherit it and refuse to leave."
      },
      {
        "id": 665,
        "type": "argument",
        "reason": "Van Toorn's PTT Calendar (1973) insists the graphic designer is a subjective author — collage as editorial position. Experimental Jetset's SMK Poster (2006) insists the graphic designer is a systematic arranger — Helvetica as objective discipline. The Crouwel/van Toorn debate continued by the next generation: system versus statement."
      },
      {
        "id": 55,
        "type": "method",
        "reason": "Vignelli's New York Subway signage (1970) uses Helvetica as a tool of public clarity — one typeface organising a transit system. Experimental Jetset uses Helvetica as a tool of intellectual clarity — one typeface organising a design practice. Both limit their palette to Helvetica and find that restriction generates, rather than limits, possibility."
      }
    ],
