    "origin": "United Kingdom",
    "manufacturer": "Alexander McQueen",
    "collection": "V&A, London (Savage Beauty exhibition)",
    "movement": "Conceptual Fashion",
    "wikiTitle": null,
    "description": "McQueen's final collection before his death in February 2010 — a vision of human evolution in reverse, imagining humanity returning to the sea as the ice caps melt. Digitally engineered prints replicate reptilian scales, jellyfish tentacles, and coral formations across structured silhouettes. The Armadillo boots — 12-inch stilettos with no visible heel — force the wearer into a crustacean-like posture. The show was the first fashion presentation livestreamed globally, crashing the servers of the platform hosting it.",
    "significance": "Plato's Atlantis proved that fashion could be speculative design — not predicting trends but proposing futures. McQueen used the runway as a platform for environmental narrative: what will bodies look like when the oceans rise? The digital prints achieved a resolution that blurred the boundary between fabric and skin. The Armadillo boots are unwearable by conventional standards, which is exactly McQueen's argument: fashion's purpose is not to dress bodies but to imagine them differently.",
    "connections": [
      {
        "id": 602,
        "type": "lineage",
        "reason": "Kawakubo's Body Meets Dress (1997) distorts the silhouette through padding — the body as sculptural question. McQueen's Plato's Atlantis (2010) distorts the silhouette through digital print and extreme footwear — the body as evolutionary question. Both refuse to dress the body as it is; both demand the body become something else."
      },
      {
        "id": 633,
        "type": "method",
        "reason": "Dunne & Raby's Placebo Project (2001) uses designed objects to provoke anxiety about invisible futures. McQueen's Plato's Atlantis (2010) uses designed garments to provoke anxiety about ecological futures. Both are speculative design — one in furniture scale, the other in body scale — both arguing that design's job is to make futures viscerally felt."
      },
      {
        "id": 713,
        "type": "zeitgeist",
        "reason": "McQueen's Plato's Atlantis (2010) and Van Herpen's Crystallization (2010) are both fashion collections from the same year that use digital technology to reimagine the body — McQueen through printed scales, Van Herpen through 3D-printed structures. Both blur the boundary between garment and organism. McQueen's collection is his last; Van Herpen's is among her first."
      }
    ],
