    "collection": "V&A, London",
    "movement": "Digital Couture",
    "wikiTitle": null,
    "description": "A dress 3D-printed in polyamide using selective laser sintering — the garment is not sewn, cut, or assembled but grown layer by layer from nylon powder fused by a laser. The form is a mesh of interlocking skeletal structures that wrap the body like an exoskeleton. Van Herpen collaborated with the 3D-printing company Materialise and architect Daniel Widrig. The dress flexes and moves with the body despite being made from rigid nylon, because the mesh is engineered to behave like chainmail.",
    "significance": "Van Herpen proved that 3D printing could produce not just prototypes but finished couture. The dress doesn't imitate fabric — it proposes a new category of body covering where structure is flexible and rigidity is worn. It launched a decade of collaboration between fashion and digital manufacturing, and established Van Herpen as the designer who most convincingly argues that technology and couture are not opposites.",
    "connections": [
      {
        "id": 678,
        "type": "zeitgeist",
        "reason": "Nervous System's Kinematics Dress (2014) uses algorithms to generate 2,279 interlocking panels printed as one piece — computation as couture. Van Herpen's Escapism dress (2011) uses laser sintering to grow an exoskeletal mesh — fabrication as couture. Both prove that digital manufacturing can produce garments, not just objects. Van Herpen pioneers; Nervous System systematises."
      },
      {
        "id": 710,
        "type": "zeitgeist",
        "reason": "McQueen's Plato's Atlantis (2010) reimagines the body through digital surface — printed scales that mimic biology. Van Herpen's Escapism (2011) reimagines the body through digital structure — printed mesh that mimics skeleton. McQueen applies technology to fabric; Van Herpen replaces fabric with technology."
      },
      {
        "id": 605,
        "type": "argument",
        "reason": "Kuramata's Miss Blanche (1988) encases roses in acrylic to freeze beauty in transparency. Van Herpen's Escapism dress (2011) encases the body in a nylon exoskeleton to freeze movement in structure. Both use synthetic materials to do what organic materials cannot: preserve the moment of transformation."
      },
      {
        "id": 323,
        "type": "material",
        "reason": "Van Herpen's Escapism Dress (2011) uses selective laser sintering to grow a polyamide exoskeleton — nylon powder fused by laser into interlocking skeletal structures that flex like chainmail. Balashova's earring (2022) uses additive manufacturing to build interlocking geometries impossible in traditional jewellery-making — digital fabrication producing forms that could never be cast or assembled. Both exploit 3D printing's indifference to geometric complexity, but van Herpen wraps a body and Balashova adorns an ear."
      }
    ],
