    "wikiTitle": "Matali_Crasset",
    "description": "A hospitality column that unfolds from a vertical cylinder into a complete guest sleeping system — a mattress pad rolls out from the base while a reading light extends from the top. When the guest leaves, it folds back into a standing tube that takes up less floor space than an umbrella stand. Crasset designed it after a friend named Jim kept crashing on her floor. The title is the use-case. The form is the answer.",
    "significance": "Crasset asked a question nobody was asking: what is the furniture of temporary hospitality? Not a sofa bed (which ruins both sofa and bed) but a dedicated sleeping-guest object that occupies near-zero space when not in use. When Jim Comes to Paris treats the unpredictability of social life as a design programme. It marks the moment French design moved from objects-for-rooms to objects-for-situations.",
    "connections": [
      {
        "id": 4,
        "type": "lineage",
        "reason": "The Castiglionis' Sella (1957) designs a stool for people who make short phone calls — behaviour observed, furniture invented. Crasset's When Jim Comes to Paris (1995) designs a column for people who host unexpected guests — behaviour observed, furniture invented. Both watch how people actually live and produce objects that serve what they see."
      },
      {
        "id": 700,
        "type": "argument",
        "reason": "Starck's Louis Ghost (2002) makes a permanent chair available to everyone through transparency and low price — democratic furniture as accessible beauty. Crasset's When Jim Comes to Paris (1995) makes a temporary bed available to everyone through folding and minimal footprint — democratic furniture as spatial economy. Both serve the same idea — design for all — but Starck gives you a chair, Crasset gives you a bed."
      },
      {
        "id": 77,
        "type": "method",
        "reason": "Propst's Action Office II (1968) redesigns the workplace by observing how people actually work — the cubicle born from behavioural study. Crasset's When Jim (1995) redesigns the home by observing how people actually socialise — the guest column born from behavioural study. Both treat specific human situations as design briefs: the office worker, the unexpected guest."
      }
    ],
