    "collection": "MoMA, New York",
    "movement": "Process-Led Design / Craft-Meets-Computation",
    "wikiTitle": null,
    "description": "A solar-powered machine that produces one object per day — wrapping coloured thread around a mould from sunrise to sunset, the thread colour changing with the light intensity throughout the day. On sunny days the object is thick and brightly coloured; on overcast days it is thin and muted. The machine starts automatically at dawn and stops at dusk. Each object is a physical record of one day's weather: a frozen graph of sunlight translated into thread.",
    "significance": "Mischer'Traxler designed a machine that designs. The human chooses the thread, builds the mechanism, and sets the mould — then the sun takes over. Each object is authored by the weather, not the designer. The practice collapses the distinction between natural process, mechanical process, and design authorship. MoMA acquired the work recognising it as the most elegant proposition in process-led design since Maurer's Zettel'z: the designer sets the conditions, then surrenders.",
    "connections": [
      {
        "id": 629,
        "type": "method",
        "reason": "Baas's Sweepers Clock (2009) uses real people sweeping to mark time — the human body as mechanism. Mischer'Traxler's Idea of a Tree (2008) uses real sunlight to wrap thread — the sun as mechanism. Both make time visible through continuous physical action; Baas uses labour, Mischer'Traxler uses photovoltaics."
      },
      {
        "id": 661,
        "type": "method",
        "reason": "Oxman's Silk Pavilion (2013) sets initial conditions and lets silkworms fabricate — the designer surrendering control to biology. Mischer'Traxler's Idea of a Tree (2008) sets initial conditions and lets sunlight fabricate — the designer surrendering control to weather. Both prove that the most interesting production happens when the designer stops deciding."
      },
      {
        "id": 670,
        "type": "zeitgeist",
        "reason": "Max Lamb's Pewter Stool (2007) casts furniture using the beach as a factory — the landscape as production site. Mischer'Traxler's Idea of a Tree (2008) wraps objects using the sky as a factory — the weather as production site. Both are process-led designers from the same generation who locate manufacturing outside the studio, in the environment."
      }
    ],
