    "wikiTitle": null,
    "description": "A film and installation documenting workers in a Chinese lighting factory performing the choreographic movements of their assembly-line routine — but without the products. The workers rehearse the gestures of inserting filaments, twisting wires, and packaging bulbs in an empty factory. Their hands move through the motions of production with nothing to produce. The title refers to the standard incandescent bulb they would normally be making.",
    "significance": "Cohen & Van Balen made the invisible labour of globalised manufacturing visible by removing the product and leaving only the bodies. The work asks: if a factory worker's gestures are choreography, who is the choreographer — the designer, the engineer, or the market? It extends the Formafantasma/Meindertsma lineage of supply-chain interrogation into performance, revealing that every product is really a dance that nobody watches.",
