    "description": "A full-scale replica of the artist's childhood home in Seoul — every door handle, light switch, electrical outlet, and window frame reproduced in translucent polyester fabric and suspended from the ceiling like a garment. The fabric architecture is accurate to the millimetre but weighs almost nothing — you can walk through its rooms. Suh has made fabric replicas of every home he has lived in: Seoul, Providence, New York, London, Berlin. Each one folds into a suitcase.",
    "significance": "Suh dissolves the boundary between architecture, textile, and memory. The fabric houses are not models or representations — they are architecture, just made from the wrong material. A house that folds into a suitcase is the immigrant's experience made physical: home is something you carry, not something you inhabit. The work argues that architecture is ultimately about memory rather than structure, and that the most permanent thing about a building is how it feels to remember it.",
