    "movement": "New Japanese Architecture",
    "wikiTitle": null,
    "description": "A single-storey glass workshop building supported by 305 randomly positioned white steel columns — some as thin as 16mm in diameter, none vertical, all leaning at different angles. The columns are so slender they look like grass stems; the building feels like standing in a forest clearing. No two columns are the same angle. The glass walls are frameless. The roof appears to float. Ishigami calculated each column's lean angle so that the cumulative structural system works despite — because of — its apparent chaos.",
    "significance": "Ishigami created a building that looks structurally impossible but is in fact meticulously engineered. The 305 columns carry precise loads at precise angles; what appears random is calculated. It pushes structural engineering to its perceptual limit — the point where architecture becomes so light it seems to have no structure at all. The KAIT Workshop is where Japanese architectural minimalism reaches its ultimate expression: a building that is mostly air.",
    "connections": [
      {
        "id": 687,
        "type": "lineage",
        "reason": "SANAA's 21st Century Museum (2004) uses curved glass walls to dissolve the boundary between inside and outside — transparency as democracy. Ishigami's KAIT Workshop (2008) uses frameless glass and hair-thin columns to dissolve the boundary between building and forest — transparency as nature. Both Japanese architects make glass buildings that barely seem to exist."
      },
      {
        "id": 835,
        "type": "method",
        "reason": "Ishigami's KAIT Workshop (2008) uses 305 white columns of varying thickness to create an interior that feels like a forest — repetition of near-identical elements producing atmosphere. De Waal's thrown cylinders (2005) use hundreds of white porcelain forms of varying size to create installations that feel like landscapes — repetition of near-identical elements producing contemplation. Both achieve spatial poetry through the accumulation of simple, similar units."
      },
      {
        "id": 59,
        "type": "argument",
        "reason": "Mies's Farnsworth House (1951) achieves transparency through eight steel columns on a geometric grid — modernist order made visible. Ishigami's KAIT Workshop (2008) achieves transparency through 305 columns at random angles — post-modernist chaos calculated invisible. Both glass-walled buildings, but Mies reveals his structure while Ishigami disguises his."
      }
    ],
