    "manufacturer": "n/a (intervention)",
    "collection": "n/a (documented work)",
    "movement": "New Aesthetic / Speculative Design",
    "wikiTitle": null,
    "description": "A circle of road markings — a solid white line surrounded by a dashed white line — painted on tarmac to trap a self-driving car. The solid line tells the car's vision system it cannot cross; the dashed line tells it there is no entry back into the circle. The car drives in and cannot drive out. The trap is a simple visual paradox — two standard road markings that create an inescapable logic loop for machine vision. Bridle is an artist and technologist whose practice investigates the politics of computation.",
    "significance": "Bridle made the invisible logic of autonomous vehicles visible through a trap you could paint with road-marking equipment. The work reveals that self-driving cars see the world through rules, not perception — and that rules can be weaponised. It's the most concise critique of algorithmic obedience ever produced: two lines of paint defeating millions of dollars of artificial intelligence. Design as exploit.",
    "connections": [
      {
        "id": 633,
        "type": "lineage",
        "reason": "Dunne & Raby's Placebo Project (2001) makes invisible electromagnetic fields perceptible through furniture — critical design revealing hidden technology. Bridle's Autonomous Trap (2017) makes invisible machine-vision rules exploitable through road markings — critical design weaponising hidden technology. Both reveal the systems we live inside; Dunne & Raby make you aware, Bridle makes them fail."
      },
      {
        "id": 13,
        "type": "argument",
        "reason": "Beck's Underground Map (1931) redesigns geography so humans can navigate a system — the diagram as human aid. Bridle's Autonomous Trap (2017) exploits road markings so machines cannot navigate a system — the diagram as machine trap. Both manipulate visual logic, but Beck enables navigation while Bridle prevents it."
      },
      {
        "id": 720,
        "type": "zeitgeist",
        "reason": "Cohen & Van Balen's 75 Watt (2013) reveals the choreography of factory labour by removing the product. Bridle's Autonomous Trap (2017) reveals the logic of autonomous driving by painting a trap. Both use minimal interventions to expose the rules that govern systems we take for granted — one removes objects, the other adds lines."
      }
    ],
