    "movement": "Biodesign",
    "wikiTitle": null,
    "description": "Textile dyeing using Streptomyces coelicolor bacteria — the microorganisms produce vivid pigments (reds, blues, purples) as a natural byproduct of their metabolism, deposited directly onto silk without water, fixing agents, or synthetic dyes. The bacteria are pipetted onto fabric and left to grow in controlled conditions for several days. The resulting patterns are determined by bacterial colony formation — no two are identical. The process uses 500 times less water than conventional textile dyeing.",
    "significance": "Chieza proved that one of fashion's most polluting processes — textile dyeing, responsible for 20% of global water pollution — can be replaced by biology that requires almost no water at all. The bacteria don't just dye; they pattern, creating organic formations that no printing technology can replicate. Faber Futures argues that biotechnology is not a future promise but a present capability — if the fashion industry is willing to surrender control of colour to living organisms.",
    "connections": [
      {
        "id": 713,
        "type": "lineage",
        "reason": "Lee's Biocouture (2014) grows garment material from bacterial cellulose — organisms producing fabric. Chieza's Living Colour (2017) dyes garment material with bacterial pigment — organisms producing colour. Lee grows the cloth; Chieza colours it. Together they propose a fashion supply chain where every material comes from a petri dish, not a factory."
      },
      {
        "id": 661,
        "type": "method",
        "reason": "Oxman's Silk Pavilion (2013) lets silkworms determine where silk goes — biology as fabrication agent. Chieza's Living Colour (2017) lets bacteria determine where pigment goes — biology as dyeing agent. Both surrender aesthetic control to living organisms and accept that the result will be unpredictable, unrepeatable, and better for it."
      },
      {
        "id": 34,
        "type": "argument",
        "reason": "Isola's Unikko (1964) prints one bold pattern industrially across fabric — colour as controlled graphic statement. Chieza's Living Colour (2017) lets bacteria deposit pigment biologically across fabric — colour as uncontrolled biological event. Both produce vibrant textile surfaces; Isola commands the colour, Chieza cultivates it."
      }
    ],
